//! member: true
//!
//! std.net.unix — the second address family: a stream socket bound to a
//! filesystem PATH instead of a host and a port (`AF_UNIX`).
//! Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **Two address calls and peer credentials.** listen(path) binds and
//! listens; connect(path) dials; peer_credentials reads the connected
//! peer's pid and effective ids. The address calls answer ordinary
//! std.net values; credentials are the one family-specific query.
//! `listen` returns a `net.Listener`, `connect` returns
//! a `net.Socket` — so `net.accept`, `net.read`/`net.write`, the byte pair,
//! `net.set_deadline`/`net.set_listener_deadline`, `net.close` and
//! `net.close_listener` serve a unix socket call for call, with the
//! semantics their own docs state. There is no unix-flavoured read, no
//! unix-flavoured close, and no second handle type. Stream operations
//! remain shared; peer_credentials is the Unix-family query.
//!
//! **Why it is a module and not two more functions in `std.net`.** wolf
//! has no overloading, so `listen` and `connect` cannot mean two things in
//! one module — the same rule that put the `f64` family in
//! `std.math.float` (API-CONVENTIONS §1). Spelling them
//! `net.listen_unix`/`net.connect_unix` would have kept one import at the
//! cost of a name that says its type in its suffix, which §1 spends
//! elsewhere. The cost of the split is stated rather than hidden: an
//! example here cannot show the accept-and-read half, because the
//! extractor imports exactly the documented module (§4's one-module note),
//! so both functions below carry PROSE examples and name the tests that
//! run the real thing — the `std.str.to_strbuf` precedent, at a whole
//! module's scale.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, never traps** (D30, §2, §14). The vocabulary is the
//! builtin tier's, adopted verbatim — std adds no translation layer:
//!
//! - `unsupported` — THE HOST does not serve the family. This is the tag
//!   the rest of the module's vocabulary exists to be distinguished from,
//!   and it is the reason `[os.net.unix]` was filed at all: a program that
//!   cannot tell "this machine has no AF_UNIX" from "that path is wrong"
//!   cannot choose a fallback transport, and a bare `io` for the first
//!   condition is exactly the answer #227 refused to ship. It is a
//!   PROPERTY OF THE HOST, not of the arguments: on a host that raises it,
//!   every call here raises it, always, for every path
//! - `exists` — a path is already there at bind. The runtime never
//!   clobbers a path it did not bind (see the cleanup posture below)
//! - `not_found` — at bind, a directory on the way to `path` is missing;
//!   at dial, there is no socket file at `path`
//! - `refused` — at dial only: there IS a socket file and nobody is
//!   listening on it. The stale-socket case, and the commonest failure a
//!   unix-domain client meets
//! - `denied` — the caller lacks the filesystem permission the call needs
//! - `io` — every other host failure, coarsened to one tag because no
//!   caller can act differently on them yet (§12's rule 3)
//!
//! **One dial shape is the KERNEL's answer and the two hosts disagree, so
//! neither row is promised for it.** A path that names something which is
//! not a socket at all is neither of the clause's two dial cases: macOS
//! answers `ENOTSOCK` and you get `io`; linux answers `ECONNREFUSED` and
//! you get `refused` (is36 found this on a CI runner and no developer's
//! machine — wolf-interp's `net_unix.rs` reads the row and accepts
//! either). Both are rows and neither is a trap, which is the part std
//! DOES promise. A program that must distinguish "not a socket" from "a
//! dead server" cannot do it from the row on both hosts, and should ask
//! `std.fs` about the path instead of the network.
//!
//! **Capability: `net` (I13)** — the same capability `std.net` carries,
//! for the same reason and with the same refusal: both functions are
//! tagged `net` in the toolchain's sandbox table and are categorically
//! refused at comptime (D33, `E0701`). A unix socket reaches no network
//! interface, but it is a socket, it is the transport a control plane
//! runs on, and a capability that changed meaning with the address family
//! would be a capability nobody could audit.
//!
//! **The cleanup posture, stated because it is the surprise.** Binding
//! CREATES a filesystem object, so this is the one std surface where a
//! socket leaves something behind. The clause's rule and the measured
//! behaviour agree on all three lanes:
//!
//! - `net.close_listener` of a unix listener UNLINKS its path. The runtime
//!   made the file, so the runtime removes it — you do not unlink it
//!   yourself after closing, and a `fs.remove` that follows a close is a
//!   `not_found` row waiting to happen
//! - `net.close` of an accepted or dialled STREAM never touches any path.
//!   A connection is not a name
//! - a process that DIES without closing leaves the file. The next bind
//!   answers `exists` — never a silent takeover — which is the
//!   nginx/haproxy posture: a stale socket is the operator's to remove.
//!   A program that OWNS its path removes it before binding, with
//!   `std.fs.remove`, and `fs.exists` is the guard that makes that
//!   idempotent
//! - the unlink is BEST EFFORT and never turns a close into a failure: a
//!   listener whose path was renamed out from under it still closes
//!   `ok` (measured), and the renamed file survives as a stale socket
//!
//! **What is deliberately absent.**
//!
//! - `remove_stale(path)` / any unlink helper — it would be
//!   `std.fs.remove` under another name, and it would put the `fs`
//!   capability on a `net` module so that an audit of either would have to
//!   read the other. The two-line idiom is in `listen`'s doc and in
//!   `tests/net/unix/cleanup.lu`; the day a program wants it as one call,
//!   it belongs to the caller or to `std.fs`, not here
//! - an abstract-namespace (`"\0name"`, linux) address — a wolf `str` is
//!   UTF-8 and the builtin takes a path; the clause names no abstract
//!   form, so std does not invent a spelling for one
//! - a `supported()` predicate — there is no builtin to ask, and a probe
//!   that binds a temporary path to find out would answer a different
//!   question (it would fold `denied` and `exists` into "no"). The row IS
//!   the predicate: call `listen`, handle `unsupported`, and the answer
//!   costs nothing on a host that serves the family
//! - `net.port` on a unix listener — it is `std.net`'s function and it
//!   answers `io` here, because a path has no port. Measured on all three
//!   lanes and pinned in `tests/net/unix/echo.lu`; nothing in this module
//!   shadows it
//! - ~~`peer_credentials`~~ — **HERE now (sc83, wolf 0.2.40)**, with
//!   Credentials below: Linux and PAX report SO_PEERCRED; macOS reports
//!   LOCAL_PEERCRED and LOCAL_PEERPID, pid -1 after the peer has closed;
//!   FreeBSD reports getpeereid and pid -1. Windows answers unsupported.
//!   lupin 0.1.62 refuses a Unix credential read at run by builtin name.
//!
//! **Paths.** §14's rule, unchanged: a path is a `str`, the separator is
//! `/`, and every test and example here uses a RELATIVE path in the
//! program's own working directory. There is a second reason beyond
//! §14's here, and it is the host's: `sun_path` is about 104 bytes on
//! macOS and 108 on linux, so an absolute path under a deep scratch
//! directory is a length failure waiting for a different machine. A
//! relative path is also the only spelling lupin serves (below).
//!
//! **Lanes (measured at the sc36 pin, wolf v0.2.4 + lupin 0.1.25).** All
//! three lanes serve the family: the checked and native rungs since s136,
//! and lupin since is36 — the reference machine has no filesystem, and
//! still binds and dials a socket path, because a unix socket is a host
//! object rather than a file the machine has to read. Its one limit is
//! stated by name: a path that is absolute or climbs out of the working
//! directory is `unsupported` at resolve there (wolf-interp#18 item 6),
//! which the relative-path discipline above already satisfies. What lupin
//! does NOT have is `std.fs`, so the two witnesses that ASSERT about the
//! path — the cleanup posture and the stale-socket `refused` row — are
//! compiler-lanes-only, and the ledger says so on those rows alone.
//!
//! **Hosts.** linux and macOS serve the family on every tier. Windows
//! answers `unsupported` at this pin, BY NAME: `AF_UNIX` has existed there
//! since Win10 1803 and the toolchain measures the kernel's answer on the
//! runner, but the runtime carries no winsock binding for it, and the
//! clause names the serving rung for a later campaign rather than
//! claiming it. A program that must run on all three hosts writes the
//! `unsupported` arm and falls back to loopback TCP; a program that has
//! no fallback should say so at startup rather than at first connection.

use std.net

/// Bind a listening socket to the filesystem path `path` and return it.
///
/// `path` is a path as the host understands it, relative and
/// forward-slashed (see the module header). The answer is an ordinary
/// `net.Listener`: `net.accept` takes connections from it,
/// `net.set_listener_deadline` arms its budget, and `net.close_listener`
/// closes it — and UNLINKS `path`, which is the one thing about this
/// listener that a TCP one does not do.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` when the HOST does not serve the family (windows at
/// this pin) — refused by name, never a bare `io`, which is the whole
/// point of the tag; `exists` when something is already at `path` (a live
/// socket, a stale one, or an ordinary file — the runtime never clobbers a
/// path it did not bind); `not_found` when a directory on the way to
/// `path` is missing; `denied` when the caller lacks permission to create
/// it; `io` for every other host failure. Never traps.
///
/// **The idiom for a path a program owns**, and it is two lines because
/// the third — deciding whether the socket at that path is somebody
/// else's — is not a decision a library can make for you:
///
///     if fs.exists(path) { fs.remove(path)? }
///     var srv = unix.listen(path)?
///
/// Removing unconditionally is right for a path this program's own
/// configuration names, and WRONG for a path an operator might be sharing;
/// `exists` is the row that lets a careful program stop instead.
///
/// The listener starts accepting immediately: a peer may connect before
/// `net.accept` is ever called and waits in the host's backlog.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_listen_unix`.
///
/// **No fenced example**: exercising a listener needs `std.net`'s
/// `accept`/`read`/`close_listener`, and the extractor imports exactly the
/// documented module (§4's one-module note) — the `std.str.to_strbuf`
/// precedent. In prose: after `var srv = unix.listen("ctl.sock")?` the
/// file `ctl.sock` exists and a peer can dial it, a second
/// `unix.listen("ctl.sock")` is `exists`, `net.port(srv)` is `io`, and
/// `net.close_listener(take srv)?` removes the file again. The runnable
/// versions are `tests/net/unix/echo.lu` (three lanes) and
/// `tests/net/unix/cleanup.lu`.
pub fn listen(path: str) -> net.Listener ! {unsupported, exists, not_found, denied, io} {
    net.Listener { fd: net_listen_unix(path)? }
}

/// Dial the unix-domain socket at `path` and return the connected socket.
///
/// The answer is an ordinary `net.Socket`, indistinguishable from a dialled
/// TCP one: `net.read`/`net.write`, the byte pair, `net.set_deadline` and
/// `net.close` all serve it, and `net.close` never touches `path` — the
/// listener owns the name, a connection does not.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` when the HOST does not serve the family (windows at
/// this pin), refused by name; `not_found` when there is no file at
/// `path`; `refused` when there IS a socket file and nobody is listening
/// on it — the stale-socket case, and the row a client meets when the
/// server died without closing; `denied` when the caller lacks permission;
/// `io` for every other host failure. Never traps.
///
/// **A `path` that names something which is not a socket at all is the
/// KERNEL's call, and the hosts differ**: macOS answers `ENOTSOCK`, which
/// arrives as `io`, and linux answers `ECONNREFUSED`, which arrives as
/// `refused` (is36's measurement, on a linux runner). It is neither of
/// the two dial cases `[os.net.unix]` rules, so std promises only what is
/// true of both: a row, never a trap. Do not branch on which.
///
/// **This blocks until the connection completes or fails.** There is no
/// name to resolve and no network to cross, so the wait is the host's
/// alone; a connect deadline has no arming surface here for `net.connect`'s
/// reason — `net.set_deadline` needs the `Socket` this call has not yet
/// produced.
///
/// Capability `net`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `net_connect_unix`.
///
/// **No fenced example**, for `listen`'s reason (§4's one-module note). In
/// prose: `unix.connect("ctl.sock")` answers a `net.Socket` when a
/// listener is bound at `ctl.sock`, `not_found` when nothing is there, and
/// `refused` when the file is a socket whose listener has gone. The
/// runnable versions are `tests/net/unix/echo.lu`,
/// `tests/net/unix/rows.lu` and `tests/net/unix/refused_row.lu`.
pub fn connect(path: str) -> net.Socket ! {unsupported, refused, not_found, denied, io} {
    net.Socket { fd: net_connect_unix(path)? }
}

/// The peer's process id, effective user id and effective group id, as
/// recorded when the Unix stream connected. pid is -1 when not available.
/// Pure data, no capability; fields are named integers, not host structs.
pub struct Credentials {
    pid: int,
    uid: int,
    gid: int,
}

/// The credentials at the other end of connected s, accepted or dialed.
/// Linux/PAX keep all three after peer close. macOS keeps uid and gid but
/// reports pid -1 after close; ask at accept if the pid is needed. FreeBSD
/// reports the effective ids and pid -1. No user database lookup is made.
///
/// Rows: io for TCP, a listener, forged/closed handles or host failure;
/// unsupported on Windows. Never traps. Capability net; refused at
/// comptime. Delegates to net_peer_cred. Both compiler tiers serve it;
/// lupin 0.1.62 refuses a live Unix stream's query by name at run.
///
/// Prose example (accept/close need std.net): after accepting a Unix
/// stream, let c = unix.peer_credentials(peer)?; compare c.uid to the
/// server's allowed effective uid before reading a request. Runnable
/// rows: tests/net/unix/peer_credentials.lu and peer_credentials_child.lu.
pub fn peer_credentials(s: net.Socket) -> Credentials ! {io, unsupported} {
    let (p, u, g) = net_peer_cred(s.fd)?
    Credentials { pid: p, uid: u, gid: g }
}
